//model answer of (3)//
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
cin.tie(nullptr);

int X, A, B, C;
cin >> X >> A >> B >> C;

int ans = INT_MAX;

for (int a = 0; a * A <= X; a++) {
    for (int b = 0; a * A + b * B <= X; b++) {
        int rest = X - a * A - b * B;
        if (rest % C == 0) {
            int c = rest / C;
            ans = min(ans, a + b + c);
        }
    }
}

if (ans == INT_MAX) cout << -1 << "\n";
else cout << ans << "\n";
}

#usually thought is “Always take as many large coins as possible”: called "Greedy algorithm"
BUT... This sometimes fails.
Counterexample
X = 10
A = 6, B = 4, C = 1

Greedy (take 6 first): 6 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 10 → 5 coins

But optimal is: 4 + 4 + 1 + 1 = 10 → 4 coins

So pure greedy is not safe.
That means:
❌ You cannot just sort coins descending and greedily take them.

2️⃣ Correct approach for this problem
Because: X ≤ 10^6

You only have 3 coin types
The intended solution is brute force on two coins + check the third.

This is O(X / max(A,B,C)), which is fast enough.

3️⃣ Key idea (important)

We try all possible counts of coin A and B, and see if the remainder can be filled with coin C.

Mathematically:

𝑎𝐴+𝑏𝐵≤𝑋

(𝑋−𝑎𝐴−𝑏𝐵)%𝐶==0
(X−aA−bB)%C==0

Then:
𝑐=(X−aA−bB)/C

Minimize: 𝑎+𝑏+𝑐

6️⃣ Mental takeaway (very important)

When you have few coin types and a bounded sum,
brute force over counts beats greedy.

This pattern appears a lot in AtCoder.

When X (or N) is big, you usually need O(N) or O(N log N).

But if one dimension is big and another dimension is tiny (here: only 3 coin types), you can often “spend” time on the small dimension by brute forcing counts.

A good rule of thumb you can use in contests:

Brute force counts when:

number of types is small (2–3),

and your loops are bounded by X / coin_value, not by X itself.



